            <ArticleTitle>[Prospective analysis of conservative treatment of acute coronary syndrome].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>34-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The in-hospital and intermediate outcomes in 341 patients with acute coronary syndrome treated with conservative (non-invasive) interventions were analysed. The coronary reperfusion was achieved in 59% patients with acute myocardial infarction (MI) treated with thrombolysis, but in 27% patients with MI treated only heparin and aspirin. In-hospital survival was 91% in 93 patients with MI treated with thrombolysis and 82% in 71 patients with MI without thrombolysis. On 6 month survival was 86% in patients with MI treated with thrombolysis, 70% in patients with MI without thrombolysis, 79% in patients with non-Q-wave MI and 88% in patients with unstable angina.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
